    overview: "The key to happiness is alertness and insight, and the spring of misery is delusion and heedlessness. The hearts of the clever are like the niche with the lamp in the verse of light. The hearts of the deluded are like layers of darkness on a deep sea. Ghazali will show how delusion enters four groups: scholars, worshippers, Sufis and the wealthy.",
    thesis: "There is no blessing greater than faith and knowledge, and no calamity greater than unbelief and sin. The deluded is the one whose inner sight never opened, so desire leads him and Satan guides him.",
    moves: [
      { title: "The One who holds the keys", body: "Ghazali begins with praise of God, in whose hand are the keys of all things, and by whose power are the keys of good and evil. He brings His friends out of darkness into light, and leads His enemies into the pits of delusion. Blessings on Muhammad, who brought creation out of the dark, and on his family and Companions. The life of this world did not delude them, and the deceiver did not delude them about God." },
      { title: "Alertness and delusion", body: "The key to happiness is alertness and insight. The spring of misery is delusion and heedlessness. God has given His servants no blessing greater than faith and knowledge, and there is no way to it except the opening of the breast by the light of insight. There is no calamity greater than unbelief and sin, and nothing calls to them except the blindness of the heart in the darkness of ignorance." },
      { title: "The niche and the lamp", body: "The hearts of the clever and the people of insight are “like a niche in which is a lamp. The lamp is in a glass. The glass is like a shining star, lit from a blessed olive tree, neither of the east nor of the west. Its oil would almost shine even if no fire touched it. Light upon light.”" },
      { title: "Darkness on a deep sea", body: "The hearts of the deluded are “like darkness on a deep sea, covered by a wave, above which is a wave, above which is a cloud. Layers of darkness, one above another. When he holds out his hand, he can hardly see it. Whoever God gives no light has no light.” The clever are those God willed to guide, so He opened their breasts to Islam and guidance. The deluded are those God willed to leave astray, so He made their breasts tight and narrow, as if they were climbing into the sky." },
      { title: "Led by desire", body: "The deluded is the one whose inner sight never opened, so he could be his own guide. He stayed blind, and took desire as his leader and Satan as his guide. “Whoever is blind in this world will be blind in the next, and further from the path.”" },
      { title: "Know where it gets in", body: "Since delusion is the mother of miseries and the spring of ruin, its ways in and its courses must be explained. The places where delusion often falls must be set out, so the seeker can beware of it once he knows it. The one given success is the one who knows the ways in of harm and corruption, guards against them, and builds his affair on firmness and insight." },
      { title: "Four groups", body: "So Ghazali explains the kinds of delusion and the groups of the deluded. They are judges, scholars and the righteous, deluded by beginnings that look beautiful outside and are ugly inside. He shows how they were deluded and what they missed. The cases are more than can be counted, but examples can point the way. The groups are many, but four kinds gather them: scholars, worshippers, Sufis, and the wealthy." },
      { title: "How it works", body: "Each kind has many groups, and the ways they are deluded differ. Some see a wrong as right, like someone who builds a mosque and decorates it with unlawful money. Some cannot tell what they work for for themselves from what they work for for God, like a preacher whose aim is acceptance and standing. Some leave what matters most and busy themselves with something else. Some leave the duty and busy themselves with the extra. Some leave the kernel and busy themselves with the husk, like someone whose whole concern in prayer is pronouncing the letters correctly. These only become clear by setting out the groups and giving examples." },
    ],
    closer: [
      { title: "Light and darkness", body: "The two verses frame the book. One heart is a lamp in glass, lit even before fire touches it. The other is a man on a stormy sea who cannot see his own hand. Delusion is not a lack of effort. It is effort in the dark." },
      { title: "The husk and the kernel", body: "The list of ways delusion works is the book in miniature. Every case that follows is one of these: a wrong seen as right, a self-serving aim seen as God’s, the lesser put before the greater, the husk before the kernel." },
    ],
    distinction: ["Two hearts", "The niche with the lamp", "The clever: light upon light.", "Darkness on a deep sea", "The deluded: cannot see his own hand."],
